MOUNT_CD9660(8)           BSD System Manager's Manual          MOUNT_CD9660(8)

NNAAMMEE
     mmoouunntt__ccdd99666600 — mount an ISO-9660 file system

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     mmoouunntt__ccdd99666600 [--bbeeggjjrrvv] [--CC _c_h_a_r_s_e_t] [--GG _g_i_d] [--mm _m_a_s_k] [--MM _m_a_s_k]
                  [--oo _o_p_t_i_o_n_s] [--ss _s_t_a_r_t_s_e_c_t_o_r] [--UU _u_i_d] _s_p_e_c_i_a_l _n_o_d_e

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The mmoouunntt__ccdd99666600 utility attaches the ISO-9660 file system residing on
     the device _s_p_e_c_i_a_l to the global file system namespace at the location
     indicated by _n_o_d_e.  This command is normally executed by mount(8) at boot
     time.

     The options are as follows:

     --bb      Relax checking for Supplementary Volume Descriptor Flags field
             which is set to a wrong value on some Joliet formatted disks.

     --ee      Enable the use of extended attributes.

     --gg      Do not strip version numbers on files.  (By default, if there are
             files with different version numbers on the disk, only the last
             one will be listed.)  In either case, files may be opened without
             explicitly stating a version number.

     --GG _g_r_o_u_p
             Set the group of the files in the file system to _g_r_o_u_p.  The de‐
             fault gid on volumes without extended attributes or Rockridge ex‐
             tensions is zero.

     --UU _u_s_e_r
             Set the owner of the files in the file system to _u_s_e_r.  The de‐
             fault uid on volumes without extended attributes or Rockridge ex‐
             tensions is zero.

     --mm _m_a_s_k
             Specify the maximum file permissions for files in the file sys‐
             tem.  For example, a _m_a_s_k of 544 limits the owner to read and ex‐
             ecute permissions for files and others to only read permission.
             See chmod(1) for more information about octal file modes.  The
             default _m_a_s_k is 7777.  The default permissions on volumes without
             extended attributes or Rockridge extensions is 555.

     --MM _m_a_s_k
             Specify the maximum file permissions for directories in the file
             system.  See the previous option's description for details.

     --jj      Do not use any Joliet extensions included in the file system.

     --oo      Options are specified with a --oo flag followed by a comma sepa‐
             rated string of options.  See the mount(8) man page for possible
             options and their meanings.  The following cd9660 specific op‐
             tions are available:

             eexxttaatttt        Same as --ee.
             ggeennss          Same as --gg.
             nnoojjoolliieett      Same as --jj.
             nnoorrrriipp        Same as --rr.
             bbrrookkeennjjoolliieett  Same as --bb.

     --rr      Do not use any Rockridge extensions included in the file system.

     --ss _s_t_a_r_t_s_e_c_t_o_r
             Start the file system at _s_t_a_r_t_s_e_c_t_o_r.  Normally, if the underly‐
             ing device is a CD-ROM drive, mmoouunntt__ccdd99666600 will try to figure out
             the last track from the CD-ROM containing data, and start the
             file system there.  If the device is not a CD-ROM, or the table
             of contents cannot be examined, the file system will be started
             at sector 0.  This option can be used to override the behaviour.
             Note that _s_t_a_r_t_s_e_c_t_o_r is measured in CD-ROM blocks, with 2048
             bytes each.  This is the same as for example the iinnffoo command of
             cdcontrol(1) is printing.  It is possible to mount an arbitrary
             session of a multi-session CD by specifying the correct
             _s_t_a_r_t_s_e_c_t_o_r here.

     --CC _c_h_a_r_s_e_t
             Specify local _c_h_a_r_s_e_t to convert Unicode file names when using
             Joliet extensions.

     --vv      Be verbose about the starting sector decisions made.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     The following command can be used to mount a Kodak Photo-CD:

           mount_cd9660 -o rw -v -s 0 /dev/cd0 /cdrom

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     cdcontrol(1), mount(2), unmount(2), cd9660(5), fstab(5), mdconfig(8),
     mount(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The mmoouunntt__ccdd99666600 utility first appeared in 4.4BSD.

     The Unicode conversion routine was added by Ryuichiro Imura
     <_i_m_u_r_a_@_r_y_u_1_6_._o_r_g> in 2003.

BBUUGGSS
     POSIX device node mapping is currently not supported.

     Version numbers are not stripped if Rockridge extensions are in use.  In
     this case, accessing files that do not have Rockridge names without ver‐
     sion numbers gets the one with the lowest version number and not the one
     with the highest.

     There is no ECMA support.

